== Chapter XII

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Two Inferences drawn from the Proposition.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

1\. #smallcaps[IT] shows us the necessity of effectual calling; without it there is no going to Heaven: We must be #emph[made meet for the inheritance,] Col 1:12. As God makes Heaven fit for us, so he makes us fit for Heaven; and what gives this idoneity and meetness, but effectual Calling? A man remaining in the filth and rubbish of nature, is no more fit for Heaven, than a dead man is fit to inherit. The #emph[High Calling] is not a thing arbitrary, or indifferent, but as needful as salvation; yet, alas, how is this one thing needful neglected! Most men, like the people of #emph[Israel,] wander up and down #emph[to gather straw,] but mind not evidences of their effectual Calling.

#footnote[2 #emph[Branch.]] 2. Take notice what a mighty power God puts forth in Calling of sinners! God doth so #emph[call] as #emph[draw,] John 6:44. Conversion is styled a #emph[Resurrection,] Rev 20:6. #emph[Blessed is he that hath part in the first Resurrection]\; that is a rising from sin to grace. A man can no more convert himself, than a dead man can raise himself. It is called a Creation, Col 3:10. To create is above the power of nature.

#footnote[#emph[Object.]] But say the #emph[Arminians,] the Will is not dead, but sleepeth, and God by a moral persuasion does only awaken us, and then the will can obey God's call and move of it self to its own conversion.

#footnote[#emph[Answ.]] To this I answer, Every man is by sin bound in fetters, Acts 8:23. #emph[I perceive thou art in the bond of iniquity.] A man that is in fetters if you use arguments, and persuade him to go, is that sufficient? There must be a beating off his fetters, and setting him free, before he can walk: So it is with every natural man, he is fettered with corruption; now the Lord by converting grace must file off his fetters, nay, give him legs to run too, or he can never obtain salvation.
